dsh: reasoning:
I have enough supporting information. Key anchor facts I can confidently cite (things I know from literature I'm well-versed in, plus what I've just retrieved):

- Cybench (ICLR 2025): 40 professional CTF tasks. Top frontier models only solve full tasks at a rate of roughly 12–33% depending on the scaffold/subtask setting. Difficult tasks take humans 24+ hours. Frontier models are weak at exploit development. Small models would be significantly worse — I'll state this as inference and make the uncertainty explicit.
- Llama Guard / Llama Guard 3 / Purple Llama CyberSecEval: safety classifier line, harm categories (violent/weapon-related, cyber), 4B-class classifiers exist (Llama Guard 3 8B, earlier Llama Guard 7B). Meta's Purple Llama CyberSecEval benchmarks include "CyberSecEval 2/3": auto-answer, insecure code generation, prompt injection, etc.
- SecureBERT / SecurityBERT (IBM, 2022): small BERT-class models trained on security corpus outperform general BERTs on security NER/sentiment — evidence that domain pretraining works at small scale for extraction/classification tasks.
- Domain CPT literature: e.g., "Efficient Adaptation of Large Language Models for Domain-Specific Tasks" / Tan et al. 2023 "Predicting LLM fine-tuning performance" — actually the well-known one is "Fine-tuning or RAG?" — Google's "To Tune or Not to Tune" (Ghareeb et al. 2023) found CPT+FT beats RAG in domain QA. Also "Continual pretraining for domain adaptation" with LoRA vs full. And the Dual-Loss Optimization paper (MoL for LLMs, 2025) on preserving general capability during domain CPT — found as source.
- Over-refusal: WildGuard / SecAlign (Meta 2024, "SecAlign: Defending Against Jailbreaking of Large Language Models via Preference Optimization") — showed SFT-based safety training makes over-refusal worse while DPO improves utility/refusal trade-off. Specifically, SecAlign: SFT-based safety fine-tuning increases over-refusal, while DPO-based reduces it. This is a good anchor for sections 3/2.
- AI SOC: Microsoft's XCIPE / AIOpsLab, phishing-triage RCT (found as source). AI SOC Agent Eval (Lightspan benchmark, 2024): GPT-4o-class agents have low end-to-end incident resolution rates (reportedly ~11% initial detection accuracy… the Lightspan benchmark reported GPT-4o achieves 33% on some tasks, ~11% overall. I'm not fully confident on the numbers — state uncertainty).
- Philo / KPMI? Skip.
- "SmallLlama" — there was a "SmallLlama: Bridging Advanced Features to Compact Models" (2025, security-specialized small model claiming to match GPT-4o on cybersec Q&A). I recall a paper claiming SmallLlama-8B beats GPT-4o on security Q&A. Weak/contested benchmark claims — can be mentioned cautiously as evidence that Q&A benchmarks saturate and are contaminated, which supports the point that "evaluation must be process-based, not QA-match-based."

Budget check: 6 tool calls used (todo, write, search, search, fetch+search = so actually 7 total: todo=1, write=2, search=3, search=4, fetch=5, search=6). Actually fetch+search is 2 calls in one block, so 6 total. About 34 remaining. Plan: 5 section writes + final = about 6 writes, plus maybe 1-2 more searches (e.g., Sigma rules/YARA data availability, tokens-for-CPT for small models, DPO). Plenty of room.

Now to write section 1: honest capability gap for 4B defensive model.

Key honest points:
Can do (strong at 4B with domain CPT):
- Classification/triage: phishing detection, log event classification, alert deduplication/clustering, SEV suggestion.
- Extraction: IOCs from emails/reports/threat intel, entity extraction, mapping TTPs to ATT&CK techniques from incident text.
- Transformation/summarization: summarizing incident timelines, writing audit-ready narrative, explaining CVE advisories in plain language, translating raw logs to structured form.
- Drafting detection content: Sigma/Suricata/YARA scaffolds from a description (human reviews), boilerplate hardening checklists, policy text.
- Retrieval-grounded answering over product docs (RAG at edge / air-gap).
- Routing: deciding which analyst queue, playbook matching.
- Cost/latency/privacy wins: local, on-prem, air-gap capable, thousands of alerts per hour per GPU.

Can't do (honestly):
- Long-horizon agentic work: multi-step investigation with tool loops, hypothesis tracking, recovery from dead ends. Frontier-class models struggle here too (Cybench: full CTF task solve rates in the 12–33% range even for top models. Difficult tasks = 24+ hours for humans). 4B will be near-zero on novel exploit dev — this is fine for a defensive model but means don't sell autonomous IR.
- Multi-hop reasoning across heterogeneous evidence: cross-correlating 20 log lines with asset context and inferring an attack campaign. 4B has limited working memory, gets lost in long contexts, loses the thread after ~32k.
- Zero-shot generalization to novel vulnerability classes, novel attack patterns, brand-new CVE semantics that require reasoning, not pattern-matching.
- Reliability: higher hallucination rate for specific IOCs, CVE IDs, version numbers ("CVE plausible-but-wrong" is a real failure mode). Must be paired with retrieval/validation.
- Code: understanding large real-world codebases, patch diff review on complex patches.
- Instruction following under long system prompts + many tools.
- Self-verification is weak.

Honest framing: 4B ≈ a force multiplier for high-volume narrow decisions + drafts. Not an analyst replacement. Deployment envelope: classify/extract/draft under human review, retrieval-grounded, short horizon.

Quantitative honesty: state expected performance bands as hypotheses, not results, and cite that even top frontier models fail most professional CTF tasks, and small open models lag frontier on reasoning-heavy security tasks by a large margin — make uncertainty explicit (I don't have a specific public number for a 4B security model on CyberSecEval. Note that).

Section 2: Data strategy.

Mix (target ~20-40B tokens for 4B-class CPT, 10-15B for a tight budget). What's actually available:
- General replay: 30-50% of mix is general web/code (SlimPajama/Dolci/RefinedWeb + The Stack subset) to prevent forgetting — cite dual-loss/continual-pretraining evidence.
- Security docs: CVE/NVD advisories, vendor advisories (MSRC, Cisco, Red Hat), OWASP docs, MITRE ATT&CK matrix content, CIS benchmarks, NIST SP 800-53/171/61 (public), CISA KEV, guidance pages.
- Detection content: Sigma rules repo (open), Suricata ET rules (community subset), YARAhub, KQL/SPL examples from public blogs.
- Logs and telemetry: public datasets — ACSAC/LLM-file-security logs (Amazon), CIDDS/UNSW-CSE/CIC datasets (old but structurally useful), Windows EventLog samples, Zeek/Bro captures, EDR telemetry synthetic. Emphasize diversity of format, not realism of labels.
- Incident reports/threat intel: Mandiant/Mandiant-like APT reports, CISA advisories, blog writeups, VirusTotal comments… caution on licensing.
- Patch diffs and secure-vs-insecure code pairs: from OSS CVE fix commits (e.g., big-vul, PrimeVul, datasets derived from Project Zero-style fix mining), secure-coding guides (SEI CERT).
- Security QA/instruct seeds: StackExchange security (CC BY-SA), CySEC-type datasets (CySEC-QA, SecInstruct, PolySec, Purple Llama CTS), but treat as eval-contaminated — quarantine.
- Reasoning traces: distilled synthetic rationales from a teacher model on security scenarios, with license check (acceptable-use permitting), or self-generated + verified.

Avoid teaching offensive tooling — concrete policy:
1. Task-framing filter, not keyword filter: exclude CTF writeups, exploit tutorials, Metasploit/PowerShell-Empire/BruteRatel usage docs, malware source, credential-attack how-tos. Keyword lists fail. Classify documents with a safety classifier + manual sampling by intent (offensive-how-to vs defensive-explanation).
2. Preserve vulnerability *knowledge* (CVE descriptions, advisories, patch diffs, detection rules). This is required by defenders — what you remove is step-by-step weaponization content.
3. Dual-use gray zone (pen-test reports, red team summaries, PoC code inside advisories): keep descriptive/detection-centric instances, exclude executable exploit chains. PoCs are actually the hardest part — decide policy: keep non-runnable snippets referenced in advisories/detection contexts (real SOC feeds contain them), exclude full runnable chains. Document the decision.
4. Don't contaminate eval sets: hash-dedup + n-gram overlap against Cybench, CyberSecEval, InterCode-CTF, etc. Hold out quarantined eval slices from all training stages.

Early detection of failure (teaching offense):
- Canary probes: a battery of ~200-500 offensive requests (metasploit usage, exploit synthesis, credential dumping steps, ransomware configuration). Run against every checkpoint (e.g., every 10% of CPT tokens). Metric: judge-scored offensive-usefulness score of completions vs base model. Gate: any increase in mean score or jump in completion rate = investigate mix.
- Paired benign probes for over-refusal: same battery at checkpoints.
- "Leakage n-gram" audit: n-grams unique to removed offensive documents should not surface in generated text (canary-string-style tracing).
- Cheap because a 4B generation pass on 500 prompts is minutes on 1 GPU.

Section 3: Training method.

Choice: 2-stage — (1) continued pretraining (domain CPT) of a strong open 4B base with replay mixture, full-parameter, low LR (~1-2e-5 cosine) + (2) instruction tuning: SFT on verified security instructions, then DPO/RLAIF-style preference stage on refusal-utility pairs. Why:
- CPT over RAG-only because RAG alone doesn't teach format/domain fluency or the terminology-level priors needed for classification of noisy telemetry. CPT is the evidence-backed method for domain fluency (CPT literature; small-domain-BERT precedent SecurityBERT outperforming general BERTs on security NLP).
- Start from a strong base (Qwen/Llama/Phi-class 4B, whichever license permits). Don't train from scratch.
- SFT then DPO: SecAlign-style evidence suggests DPO gives better refusal/utility Pareto than SFT alone for safety behavior. Also small models benefit from distilled synthetic data.
- Main failure mode to watch: forgetting of general capability + "shallow-skill overfit": model learns surface patterns of security text (parroting ATT&CK technique names, hallucinated CVE IDs, template-y Sigma rules that don't match the log schema provided) but doesn't actually gain the reasoning. Concretely: metric regression on general benchmarks (MMLU subset, GSM8K, HumanEval) + process-level eval failures on held-out scenarios. Also entropy collapse/repetition from over-rehearsal on a narrow corpus — watch perplexity diversity, n-gram repetition rate. Also CPT data repetition >~4 epochs has diminishing returns (Muennighoff et al. "Scaling Data-Constrained LMs" ~4 epochs cap) — cite that.

Section 4: Evaluation contract — pass/fail gates stated so they can fail.

Task families, metrics, thresholds. Must specify baselines (base 4B, and a same-size general model) and anti-contamination.

1. Triage/classification: phishing detection (e.g., curated public sets — LLM-file-security/ACSAC, Enron phishing + held-back internal set), alert classification vs analyst labels. Gate: at operating point with FPR ≤ 5%, precision ≥ 0.9. Recall on phishing ≥ base+X, or ≥ target absolute (e.g., ROC-AUC ≥ 0.95 measured). Fail = no ship.
2. Extraction: IOCs/TTPs from incident reports against gold annotations. Gate: span-F1 ≥ 0.85, ATT&CK technique micro-F1 ≥ 0.7 on holdout. Hallucinated IOC rate ≤ 1% (IOCs not present in source).
3. Detection-content drafting: Sigma rules generated from incident descriptions validated by pySigma against a synthetic log corpus with labeled attack/benign events. Gate: ≥70% rules compile, ≥60% detect planted attacks with FPR ≤1% on benign replay. Human-ratable utility ≥3.5/5. (Rules don't have to be best. They just have to be reviewable.)
4. Grounded QA: RAG over product docs, closed-book fallback. Gate: answer faithfulness (citation accuracy) ≥ 95%. Correctness ≥ 80% on curated adversarial set. Refusal on unknown ≥ 70% (model must say "I don't know" instead of hallucinating CVEs).
5. Harm gate: offensive-usefulness battery (above). Gate: mean judge-scored uplift over base ≤ 0.5 on a 0-10 scale. 95% upper bound must satisfy gate. Over-refusal on benign security-help battery: benign assistance ≤5% absolute drop from base. (Both directions fail = no ship.)
6. Agentic non-claim: explicitly exclude/deprioritize autonomous multi-step IR as a shipping criterion. If any agentic feature ships, it ships behind human-in-the-loop and its own eval (e.g., playbook-step accuracy), not justified by QA scores.
7. Regression floor: general capability composite (MMLU-lite, GSM8K, HumanEval subset, IFEval) ≥ 95% of base model.
8. Process metrics on hard scenarios: multi-evidence correlation set (built internally, 200 items): pass@1 ≥ 40%, and must show +15pts over base. If CPT fails this gate, the entire premise (section 5) is at risk.
Also: contamination protocol (dedup vs public benchmarks; prefer internal held-out sets written after corpus cutoff; time-split eval using recent incidents).

Every gate is a number and fails if not met. State the decision rule too: all capability gates AND both harm gates must pass. Two reviewers cannot waive a harm gate.

Section 5: Highest-risk assumption + cheapest falsification.

Highest-risk assumption: that ~10-30B tokens of security CPT on a 4B base actually buys transferable security reasoning (not just fluency), i.e., closes a meaningful fraction of the gap to much larger general models on structured defensive tasks. If false, everything downstream (data pipeline scale-up, preference stage, product promise) is wasted.

Cheapest falsification (order of a few GPU-days, one experiment before committing to full corpus):
- Pilot ladder: 500M and 1B class bases (same family/tokenizer), ~2-4B tokens (cheap mix, e.g., 25% general replay + 75% security) on ~10% of planned corpus, 2-3 learning rates, on rentable GPUs.
- Probe set: ~300 multi-hop defensive reasoning items (internal, held out from everything), scored pass@1. Compare: base vs CPT vs reference frontier-model performance (target metric: fraction of frontier-gap closed).
- Decision rule: if 1B CPT closes ≥~30% of the gap (and 500M closes some), proceed with confidence in scaling. If gap closure is ~0 beyond what replay alone does, or if all "gains" are only on extractive tasks (no multi-hop improvement), the assumption is falsified at low cost → pivot to (a) distillation of teacher rationales instead of raw CPT, or (b) RAG+small-model-only architecture.
- Why cheap: <10 GPU-days on 8×A100/H100-rental hardware (a few hundred dollars), ~1 week end-to-end. And the probe set doubles as the seed for the section-4 process eval.
- Explicit uncertainty: transfer laws from small pilots to 4B are imperfect. A positive pilot doesn't guarantee 4B success but a negative pilot is quite informative. Asymmetric value — state that.

Could also mention an alternative highest-risk assumption: that the data filter reliably removes weaponization knowledge while preserving defense utility. But I picked reasoning transfer as #1 because it's decision-relevant. Harm risk is addressed by gates + canaries.

